[Music]  our series of more perfect union aims to  show that what unites us  as americans is far greater than what  divides us  this morning that message has found a  home with basketball fanatics  cbs sunday morning correspondent lee  cowan introduces  us to a team creating hoops to connect  communities  and open a dialogue for social change  the venice beach basketball courts their  haven for pickup ballers  players come from everywhere to shoot  the venice vibe  until kovid shut it all down  that sent sparks flying for one player  who had suddenly lost his favorite  outlet  this is thin line between being safe and  sane  you know our motto is the game never  stops that's the way we connect  my favorite baseball baseball nick ansem  who goes by nico naysmith  founded the venice basketball league  but he's also an artist who grew up  outside paris  his twin passions found a home at the  end of a welding torch  deftly wielded by fellow artist and  friend lori powers  together they created what they call  survival hoops  we built about a hundred of them and we  were together every day  making hoops the hoop will go right in  here they're all made from reclaimed  items  chairs shoes even car bumpers  you just see a hoop in just about  everything i see a hoop and everything  you could put a few things on the ground  there and i could just immediately  make a hoop out of it  they've hung them in back alleys all  over venice  played them too downtown  but this is their real hoop dream an old  school bus  turned into a rolling court it was built  to bring venice street ball  to streets outside venice  when those streets filled up with people  protesting racism and police violence  nico thought his hoop bus should hit the  road too  like 2020 is cancelled i'm like wow but  i have this bus  and it's it's funded by the people it's  powered by the people  i'm like let's let's use what we created  together  to make change the hoop bus became a  magnet  protester even police were drawn to it  in unity did you ever  wonder what a guy who largely grew up in  france right how he he ended up being in  the middle of a black lives matter  protest  yeah no no i really don't think about it  the thing is like we're fighting for the  same cause we're fighting for justice  we're all one i'm just like doing what  what feels right  for the past month the hoop bus has been  on a cross-country road trip  stopping along the way to preach the  gospel of basketball and its ability  to bring strangers together it's a  mobile art gallery from top to bottom  pre-hester is one of the artists making  the track that's  him working on a painting on the roof  this  is his artwork too it was just amazing  to see how many people like  from every ethnicity every race  uh it was just phenomenal he too was  targeted by police who mistook him for  someone else when he was painting a  mural  on a wall a few years ago that makes  this pilgrimage  personal i was like george floyd  face down and a knee was to my back and  there was eight cops with guns drawn to  me  the youngest among us are most inspired  like 11 year old dash  corwin he made his own blm bucket i feel  like that it shows that  everybody can speak up even if you're a  kid  even if it's a small thing it still  helps a lot  now the hoop bus isn't going to end  racism but  nico naysmith hopes that it's a reminder  that victory comes  when everyone on the team is shooting  for the same goal  you see the back of the bus says vote  this is a beautiful country that has a  lot of opportunity but  if there's something you don't like like  take action on it for cbs this morning  i'm lee count in venice beach california  hello lee cowan i love everything about  that story everything me too  starting with the hoop buzz i know what  you're going to say and everyone's  shooting on the same goal yeah you know  i love that shooting for the same goal  that's right i mean that's what we need  in this country  shared reality and a shared goal i love  the black lives matter sign at the big  at the front and then the boat at the  back i love that he saw the opportunity  that he had this bus park there and he  could do something with it yeah  and a little boy dash who said it  doesn't matter your age you can all do  something  go dash

